1
00:00:03,500 --> 00:00:04,042
Welcome.

2
00:00:04,042 --> 00:00:11,294
In this course we'll take the OpenBCI Cyton board, and its add-on, the Daisy module, apart, piece by piece.

3
00:00:11,294 --> 00:00:17,528
Together they turn the tiny electrical activity of your brain into numbers on your computer:

4
00:00:17,528 --> 00:00:20,985
8 channels with the Cyton alone, 16 with the Daisy.

5
00:00:20,985 --> 00:00:23,831
You don't need any electronics background.

6
00:00:23,831 --> 00:00:33,590
We'll use a 3D model built from OpenBCI's own design files, plus the real schematics, and follow the electricity as it flows through the boards.

7
00:00:34,800 --> 00:00:36,397
Here's the big picture.

8
00:00:36,397 --> 00:00:46,186
Electrodes on your scalp pick up voltages of just 10 to 100 microvolts, tens of thousands of times smaller than the voltage of an AA battery.

9
00:00:46,186 --> 00:00:56,669
The Cyton protects those signals, amplifies them, turns them into numbers 250 times a second, and sends them by radio to a USB dongle on your computer.

10
00:00:56,669 --> 00:01:03,611
We'll follow five flows: power, the brain signal, the bias loop, the digital control, and the radio.

11
00:01:03,611 --> 00:01:08,610
Then we'll add the Daisy, and see how two boards stay perfectly in sync.
